# i* Strategic Dependency (SD) — Actors and Dependencies
**Author:** Yaseen

I identified the main actors and listed what each depends on for Use Case 1. I based this on our Sprint 1 stakeholder analysis, the case study and our two chosen functionalities. This list will help draw the SD diagram.

## Actors

- **Producer:** Submits product details and updates stock and seasonal availability.
- **Marketplace System:** Validates and publishes products, checks certification, and manages availability.
- **Marketplace Administrator:** Oversees listings and handles cases needing manual review.
- **External Organic Certification Verification Service:** Checks organic claims (external or simulated service).
- **Customer:** Uses product details and availability information.

**Scope:** Use Case 1 — Product Listing and Inventory Management. The payment gateway and 5% commission relate mainly to wider checkout/payment functionality.

## Dependencies

Format: **ID | Depender → Dependee | Dependum [type] | Why this dependency exists / traceability**

### A. Product submission and validation

**SD-01 | Producer → Marketplace System | Product successfully listed [Goal]**  
The producer relies on the platform to turn a valid submission into a discoverable product listing. (Use Case 1: publication after validation; Functionality A.)

**SD-02 | Marketplace System → Producer | Complete product submission data [Resource]**  
The platform needs product name, description, category, price, quantity, availability period, farm origin, and relevant harvest, allergen, certification and best-before details from the producer. (Use Case 1; stakeholder matrix BR-01.)

**SD-03 | Producer → Marketplace System | Clear validation feedback [Resource]**  
If submitted information is missing or invalid, the producer needs actionable feedback explaining what to correct. (Use Case 1 validation-failure path; case-study analysis usability needs.)

**SD-04 | Marketplace System → Producer | Corrected product information [Resource]**  
The system relies on the producer to correct invalid or missing details before publication can proceed. (Use Case 1; Functionality A correction loop.)

**SD-05 | Marketplace Administrator → Marketplace System | Accurate and compliant product catalogue [Goal]**  
Operations relies on the platform's checks and publication rules to maintain catalogue quality and reduce misleading product information. (Stakeholder matrix; LESP analysis.)

### B. Organic certification and exception handling

**SD-06 | Marketplace System → External Organic Certification Verification Service | Organic claim checked [Task]**  
Where a producer claims organic status, the system relies on an external/simulated verifier to assess the claim. (Use Case 1; stakeholder matrix BR-02; Functionality A.)

**SD-07 | Marketplace System → External Organic Certification Verification Service | Certification verification result [Resource]**  
The system needs a result indicating whether the claimed certificate is valid, invalid, expired or unable to be checked. This is distinct from the verification activity itself. (Functionality A; external-service failure requirement.)

**SD-08 | External Organic Certification Verification Service → Marketplace System | Certification details submitted for checking [Resource]**  
The verifier needs the relevant submitted licence/claim details to assess a request. This represents the service interaction, not a claim that the certifier depends on the marketplace for its wider organisational goals. (Stakeholder matrix BR-02; proposed API interaction.)

**SD-09 | Marketplace System → Marketplace Administrator | Unresolved verification case reviewed [Task]**  
If the verification service is unavailable or a case cannot be resolved automatically, the platform relies on administrator intervention under the group's proposed exception policy. (Functionality A; stakeholder matrix BR-02.)

**SD-10 | Producer → Marketplace System | Trustworthy organic-claim status [Goal]**  
The producer needs the platform to display organic status only when justified, or to communicate that verification is pending/rejected. (Use Case 1; LESP analysis.)

### C. Inventory and seasonal availability

**SD-11 | Producer → Marketplace System | Product stock and seasonal availability maintained [Goal]**  
The producer relies on the platform to reflect inventory and seasonal changes without manual catalogue management. (Use Case 1; Functionality B.)

**SD-12 | Marketplace System → Producer | Updated quantity and seasonal dates [Resource]**  
The system relies on producer-supplied replenishment quantities and updated availability information. (Use Case 1; stakeholder matrix BR-04; Functionality B.)

**SD-13 | Producer → Marketplace System | Out-of-stock products made unavailable [Goal]**  
The producer relies on automatic removal from customer availability when stock reaches zero, reducing overselling. (Use Case 1; stakeholder matrix BR-03.)

**SD-14 | Producer → Marketplace System | Eligible replenished products made available again [Goal]**  
The producer relies on the platform to republish replenished items **only when still within their permitted seasonal availability window**. (Use Case 1; stakeholder matrix BR-04.)

**SD-15 | Marketplace Administrator → Marketplace System | Reliable inventory and seasonal status [Goal]**  
Operations depends on the system to apply stock and seasonal rules consistently, including withdrawing expired products. (Stakeholder matrix; Functionality B.)

### D. Customer trust and marketplace outcomes

**SD-16 | Customer → Marketplace System | Accurate product information [Resource]**  
Customers need category, origin, price, harvest/best-before information where relevant, allergen details and organic-claim status to make informed choices. (Case study customer requirements; Use Case 1; LESP.)

**SD-17 | Customer → Marketplace System | Current product availability [Goal]**  
Customers depend on the platform to show only products that can currently be offered, taking account of stock and seasonality. (Use Case 1; Functionality B.)

**SD-18 | Customer → Marketplace System | Trustworthy food safety and provenance information [Softgoal]**  
Customers expect confidence in allergen declarations, local origin and organic claims. This is a quality expectation supported by validation and verification, not a guarantee that every product is risk-free. (Case study; stakeholder matrix; LESP.)

**SD-19 | Producer → Marketplace System | Easy-to-use product management [Softgoal]**  
Producers with varied digital skills depend on a usable submission/restock experience that reduces manual work. (Case study producer profile; case-study analysis.)